   "spoken": "...instead you must play d6 — a super tricky sideline. e5 is no longer dangerous, because after we take on e5 White can take on d8: we lose castling rights, but White gets no real compensation. Don't fear a bishop to e3 and queenside castling with a check — the engine just ignores it and hits the bishop with a knight to g4. And after a bishop to b5 threat, don't fear one-move threats: a bishop capture on e3 with check, dropping to d4 and intercepting the rook, wins two pieces.",
